  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the sum of all pairwise differences between outcomes of the two lotteries and prefers the lottery with the more favorable (presumably larger positive) sum. This involves comparing outcome levels across lotteries in a structural way, independent of probabilities. However, the individual in the verbal report focuses solely on the probability of achieving a positive outcome, not on outcome magnitudes or their pairwise differences. There is no mention of comparing specific amounts (e.g., 6000 vs. 4000 or 2000), nor any indication of evaluating the spread or differences between outcomes. The justification is probabilistic and binary (gain vs. no gain), whereas the decision reason is structural and magnitude-based. Therefore, the information considered by the individual (probability of positive outcome) does not align with the information required by the decision reason (pairwise outcome differences). Similarly, the justification (favoring higher chance of profit) does not match the rule of the decision reason (favoring more favorable sum of differences). Hence, it is unlikely the individual used this reason.",
